35. Population Growth: Demographic Transition and the Malthusian Model
Paper: Economics of Growth and Development I (ECNCC302) | Unit: V | Topic: World population growth through history; demographic transition; economic theories of population growth (Malthus, optimum theory, microeconomic theory of household)
Introduction
World population took until about 1800 to reach one billion, and is now over eight billion. Understanding why population grows, and what it means for development, is central to development economics.
Population growth through history
Population grew very slowly before 1750 (below 0.1 per cent a year) because high births were offset by high deaths. The Industrial Revolution, public health, sanitation and medicine lowered mortality first in Europe, then in developing regions after 1945, leading to a population explosion with growth rates above 2 per cent in the 1960s. Growth has since slowed; it is now about 1 per cent per year.
Demographic transition
Four stages:
1. Pre-transition: high birth and death rates, stable population.
2. Early transition: death rates fall, birth rates stay high, so rapid growth.
3. Late transition: birth rates fall (urbanisation, education, female literacy, contraception, child cost), growth slows.
4. Post-transition: low birth and death rates, stable or declining population (with ageing). A fifth stage of below-replacement fertility is observed in Japan and Europe.
The demographic dividend arises when the working-age share rises. India is in the late stages.
Malthusian model
In Essay on the Principle of Population (1798), Malthus argued that population grows geometrically while food grows arithmetically. Population outruns subsistence until checked by positive checks (famine, disease, war) and preventive checks (moral restraint). Income per capita is held at subsistence, a "low-level equilibrium trap" (Nelson). Criticisms: technological progress, falling fertility with rising income, and trade have invalidated the pessimism; but the model fits pre-industrial history and warns about resource limits.
Optimum theory of population
Cannan and Robbins: for given resources and technology, there is an optimum population that maximises income per head. Below it (under-population) returns increase; above it (over-population) returns diminish. The optimum changes with technology. Difficulty: hard to measure; ignores non-economic factors.
Microeconomic theory of household fertility
Becker and Leibenstein: children are treated as consumption or investment goods. Parents compare costs (time, education, forgone earnings of women) and benefits (labour, old-age security, utility). Rising income and women's wages raise the opportunity cost of children and shift preference toward quality over quantity (Becker). In poor countries, children provide labour and security, so fertility is high. Policy: raise education, female employment, child survival and pensions.
Conclusion
Population is both a source of labour and a pressure on resources. Policies that raise education, health and women's empowerment speed the transition and capture the dividend.